    shortDescription: "Raster graphics are made of a fixed grid of pixels, so they're resolution-dependent - enlarging a raster image beyond its native resolution makes it blurry or pixelated. Vector...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Raster graphics are made of a fixed grid of pixels, so they're resolution-dependent - enlarging a raster image beyond its native resolution makes it blurry or pixelated. Vector graphics are defined by mathematical paths, curves, and fills rather than pixels, so they can be scaled to any size without losing sharpness. Raster is the natural format for photographs and detailed, continuous-tone imagery; vector is preferred for logos, icons, and illustrations that need to work cleanly at many different sizes, from a favicon to a billboard.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nRaster graphics are made of a fixed grid of pixels, so they're resolution-dependent - enlarging a raster image beyond its native resolution makes it blurry or pixelated. Vector graphics are defined by mathematical paths, curves, and fills rather than pixels, so they can be scaled to any size without losing sharpness. Raster is the natural format for photographs and detailed, continuous-tone imagery; vector is preferred for logos, icons, and illustrations that need to work cleanly at many different sizes, from a favicon to a billboard.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA company logo should be maintained as vector artwork (typically an .ai, .eps, or .svg file) so it can be resized for a business card, a website header, and a building sign without ever becoming pixelated. A campaign photograph, by contrast, is inherently raster and is usually delivered as a high-resolution .jpg or .tiff.",
