    shortDescription: "RGB is an additive color model (red, green, blue light combining toward white) used for screens and digital displays. CMYK is a subtractive model (cyan, magenta, yellow, black...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "RGB is an additive color model (red, green, blue light combining toward white) used for screens and digital displays. CMYK is a subtractive model (cyan, magenta, yellow, black ink absorbing light) used for most commercial printing. Because RGB has a wider color gamut than CMYK, some vivid on-screen colors - especially bright blues, greens, and certain oranges - have no exact CMYK equivalent and will look duller once printed, which is why important brand colors should be checked against a printed proof or a CMYK/Pantone reference before finalizing, not assumed to translate automatically.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nRGB is an additive color model (red, green, blue light combining toward white) used for screens and digital displays. CMYK is a subtractive model (cyan, magenta, yellow, black ink absorbing light) used for most commercial printing. Because RGB has a wider color gamut than CMYK, some vivid on-screen colors - especially bright blues, greens, and certain oranges - have no exact CMYK equivalent and will look duller once printed, which is why important brand colors should be checked against a printed proof or a CMYK/Pantone reference before finalizing, not assumed to translate automatically.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA brand's signature bright teal looks vibrant on screen in RGB, but the closest achievable CMYK mix prints noticeably duller. A designer catches this early by soft-proofing the file in CMYK (or getting a physical proof from the printer) before the print run, rather than discovering the mismatch after thousands of brochures are printed.",
