    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "The commonly cited principles are balance (visual weight distributed intentionally, symmetrically or asymmetrically), contrast (differences in size, color, or weight that create emphasis and readability), alignment (elements positioned in relation to a consistent grid or edge, which creates order), proximity (related elements grouped together, unrelated elements separated, which communicates relationships without needing labels), repetition (consistent visual patterns that build cohesion across a design or system), and hierarchy (guiding the eye to what matters most, in what order). These aren't independent rules to check off; they interact - for example, hierarchy is usually achieved through some combination of contrast, scale, and placement together, not any single principle alone.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe commonly cited principles are balance (visual weight distributed intentionally, symmetrically or asymmetrically), contrast (differences in size, color, or weight that create emphasis and readability), alignment (elements positioned in relation to a consistent grid or edge, which creates order), proximity (related elements grouped together, unrelated elements separated, which communicates relationships without needing labels), repetition (consistent visual patterns that build cohesion across a design or system), and hierarchy (guiding the eye to what matters most, in what order). These aren't independent rules to check off; they interact - for example, hierarchy is usually achieved through some combination of contrast, scale, and placement together, not any single principle alone.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA poster uses a large, bold headline (contrast and hierarchy), consistent margins for all text blocks (alignment), related event details grouped tightly together while the sponsor logos sit in a separate cluster (proximity), and the same accent color used for the headline and the call-to-action button (repetition) to visually connect the two most important elements.",
