    shortDescription: "Visual hierarchy is the deliberate ordering of elements so a viewer's eye is guided to what matters most first, then to supporting information, in an order that matches the...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Visual hierarchy is the deliberate ordering of elements so a viewer's eye is guided to what matters most first, then to supporting information, in an order that matches the actual priority of the content - not just an aesthetic layering. It's established through size (larger elements draw attention first), color and contrast (a bright or high-contrast element stands out against a muted background), placement (elements higher or more central are typically seen first, following common reading patterns), and whitespace (isolating an element from its surroundings makes it more prominent by contrast). A design lacking hierarchy makes everything compete for attention at once, which usually means nothing gets read in the intended order.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nVisual hierarchy is the deliberate ordering of elements so a viewer's eye is guided to what matters most first, then to supporting information, in an order that matches the actual priority of the content - not just an aesthetic layering. It's established through size (larger elements draw attention first), color and contrast (a bright or high-contrast element stands out against a muted background), placement (elements higher or more central are typically seen first, following common reading patterns), and whitespace (isolating an element from its surroundings makes it more prominent by contrast). A design lacking hierarchy makes everything compete for attention at once, which usually means nothing gets read in the intended order.\n\n**Example:**\n\nOn a landing page, the headline is the largest and highest-contrast element (seen first), the supporting subheadline is smaller but still prominent, and the call-to-action button uses a distinct accent color and generous surrounding whitespace to stand out even though it isn't the largest element on the page.",
