    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Typography is the craft of arranging type to make written content both legible and expressive of the right tone - it covers typeface selection, size, spacing (kerning, tracking, leading), and hierarchy across headings and body text. Effective font pairing typically combines fonts with enough contrast to be visually distinct (a serif heading with a sans-serif body, or a heavy display weight with a lighter body weight) while sharing some underlying compatibility in proportions or mood, so they feel intentionally chosen together rather than randomly combined. A common practical rule is limiting a design to two, occasionally three, typefaces total, using weight and size variation within those families for further hierarchy rather than introducing more typefaces.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nTypography is the craft of arranging type to make written content both legible and expressive of the right tone - it covers typeface selection, size, spacing (kerning, tracking, leading), and hierarchy across headings and body text. Effective font pairing typically combines fonts with enough contrast to be visually distinct (a serif heading with a sans-serif body, or a heavy display weight with a lighter body weight) while sharing some underlying compatibility in proportions or mood, so they feel intentionally chosen together rather than randomly combined. A common practical rule is limiting a design to two, occasionally three, typefaces total, using weight and size variation within those families for further hierarchy rather than introducing more typefaces.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA brand pairs a bold, geometric sans-serif for headlines with a more neutral, highly legible sans-serif (or a classic serif) for body copy - providing enough contrast between the two to create clear hierarchy, while both remaining clean enough not to clash stylistically.",
