    shortDescription: "Kerning adjusts the spacing between two specific adjacent letters to correct for awkward visual gaps (e.g., the space between 'A' and 'V' often needs manual tightening)....",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Kerning adjusts the spacing between two specific adjacent letters to correct for awkward visual gaps (e.g., the space between 'A' and 'V' often needs manual tightening). Tracking adjusts the spacing uniformly across a whole range of characters or an entire block of text, used for overall density or a stylistic effect (like widely-tracked all-caps headings). Leading is the vertical space between lines of text (baseline to baseline), and getting it wrong - too tight or too loose - significantly hurts readability even if individual letterforms and their spacing look fine.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nKerning adjusts the spacing between two specific adjacent letters to correct for awkward visual gaps (e.g., the space between 'A' and 'V' often needs manual tightening). Tracking adjusts the spacing uniformly across a whole range of characters or an entire block of text, used for overall density or a stylistic effect (like widely-tracked all-caps headings). Leading is the vertical space between lines of text (baseline to baseline), and getting it wrong - too tight or too loose - significantly hurts readability even if individual letterforms and their spacing look fine.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA logo's wordmark often needs manual kerning between specific letter pairs to look balanced, a stylized all-caps headline might use increased tracking for a more spacious, premium feel, and a body-text paragraph needs leading set to roughly 120-145% of the type size for comfortable reading - three distinct adjustments serving different purposes.",
