    sampleAnswer: "Photoshop is a raster (pixel-based) editor built for photo editing, compositing, and detailed pixel-level image manipulation. Illustrator is a vector editor built for scalable artwork - logos, icons, illustrations, and typography-driven layouts - where geometric precision and infinite scalability matter more than photographic detail. The deciding factor is the nature of the final output: if the work involves photographic imagery, retouching, or pixel-level effects, Photoshop is the right tool; if the work needs to scale cleanly across sizes or be edited as discrete, resizable shapes, Illustrator is the right tool. Many real projects use both - a logo built in Illustrator might be composited over a photo edited in Photoshop for a final marketing asset.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPhotoshop is a raster (pixel-based) editor built for photo editing, compositing, and detailed pixel-level image manipulation. Illustrator is a vector editor built for scalable artwork - logos, icons, illustrations, and typography-driven layouts - where geometric precision and infinite scalability matter more than photographic detail. The deciding factor is the nature of the final output: if the work involves photographic imagery, retouching, or pixel-level effects, Photoshop is the right tool; if the work needs to scale cleanly across sizes or be edited as discrete, resizable shapes, Illustrator is the right tool. Many real projects use both - a logo built in Illustrator might be composited over a photo edited in Photoshop for a final marketing asset.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA poster combining a retouched photograph with a scalable logo and clean typographic layout would typically involve retouching the photo in Photoshop, then bringing it into Illustrator (or InDesign, for longer layouts) alongside the vector logo and text for final composition.",
