    shortDescription: "JPG uses lossy compression and no transparency support, making it well-suited for photographs where some quality loss is imperceptible and file size matters. PNG supports...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "JPG uses lossy compression and no transparency support, making it well-suited for photographs where some quality loss is imperceptible and file size matters. PNG supports lossless compression and transparency, making it the standard choice for graphics, icons, or images needing a transparent background, though at a larger file size than an equivalent JPG. SVG is a vector format (XML-based, describing shapes mathematically rather than as pixels) ideal for logos, icons, and illustrations on the web since it scales perfectly at any size and resolution with a small file size. PDF is a versatile, largely print-oriented format that can embed both vector and raster content along with fonts, commonly used for print-ready files and multi-page documents because it preserves layout fidelity across different systems.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nJPG uses lossy compression and no transparency support, making it well-suited for photographs where some quality loss is imperceptible and file size matters. PNG supports lossless compression and transparency, making it the standard choice for graphics, icons, or images needing a transparent background, though at a larger file size than an equivalent JPG. SVG is a vector format (XML-based, describing shapes mathematically rather than as pixels) ideal for logos, icons, and illustrations on the web since it scales perfectly at any size and resolution with a small file size. PDF is a versatile, largely print-oriented format that can embed both vector and raster content along with fonts, commonly used for print-ready files and multi-page documents because it preserves layout fidelity across different systems.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA website's logo is delivered as an SVG (scales perfectly across all screen sizes and pixel densities), a photo gallery uses JPGs (smaller file sizes for photographic content), a UI icon set with transparent backgrounds uses PNGs, and the final print-ready brochure is exported as a PDF with embedded fonts and correct color settings.",
