    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Print files typically need CMYK color mode (or a specified spot/Pantone color where exact brand color matching matters), 300 DPI resolution at final physical size, bleed and safe margins built in, and fonts either embedded or converted to outlines to avoid font-substitution issues at the print vendor. Screen files use RGB color mode, resolution defined in pixels relative to the target display (often accounting for multiple pixel densities like @1x/@2x/@3x for different screen types), and optimized, compressed file formats appropriate for fast loading (SVG for vector graphics, compressed JPG/WebP for photos, PNG for transparency). Getting these settings wrong in either direction - sending an RGB, 72 DPI file to a commercial printer, or shipping an unoptimized 300 DPI CMYK file to a website - causes real, avoidable production problems.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPrint files typically need CMYK color mode (or a specified spot/Pantone color where exact brand color matching matters), 300 DPI resolution at final physical size, bleed and safe margins built in, and fonts either embedded or converted to outlines to avoid font-substitution issues at the print vendor. Screen files use RGB color mode, resolution defined in pixels relative to the target display (often accounting for multiple pixel densities like @1x/@2x/@3x for different screen types), and optimized, compressed file formats appropriate for fast loading (SVG for vector graphics, compressed JPG/WebP for photos, PNG for transparency). Getting these settings wrong in either direction - sending an RGB, 72 DPI file to a commercial printer, or shipping an unoptimized 300 DPI CMYK file to a website - causes real, avoidable production problems.\n\n**Example:**\n\nThe same brochure artwork, prepared for print, is exported as a CMYK PDF with bleed at 300 DPI and outlined fonts; a web version of the same visual is separately exported as RGB, web-optimized PNG/SVG assets at screen-appropriate pixel dimensions - two different export pipelines from the same source file, not one file used for both purposes.",
