    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Standard CMYK process printing mixes four inks and can produce slight color variation between print runs or printers, which is a problem when exact, consistent brand color...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Standard CMYK process printing mixes four inks and can produce slight color variation between print runs or printers, which is a problem when exact, consistent brand color matters (a brand's signature red needs to look identical on packaging printed by different vendors). Pantone (PMS) spot colors are pre-mixed, standardized inks matched to a physical swatch book, giving much more consistent and predictable results than a CMYK approximation, though at a higher printing cost since spot colors typically require an additional ink plate rather than reusing the standard four-color process. The practical decision is whether exact color consistency is worth the added production cost and complexity for that specific project - often yes for a logo or core brand element, less often for a one-off flyer.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStandard CMYK process printing mixes four inks and can produce slight color variation between print runs or printers, which is a problem when exact, consistent brand color matters (a brand's signature red needs to look identical on packaging printed by different vendors). Pantone (PMS) spot colors are pre-mixed, standardized inks matched to a physical swatch book, giving much more consistent and predictable results than a CMYK approximation, though at a higher printing cost since spot colors typically require an additional ink plate rather than reusing the standard four-color process. The practical decision is whether exact color consistency is worth the added production cost and complexity for that specific project - often yes for a logo or core brand element, less often for a one-off flyer.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA beverage brand's can label uses a Pantone spot color for its signature brand red to guarantee it looks identical whether printed by any of several co-packing plants, while the full-color product photography on the same label is still reproduced using standard CMYK process printing, since photographic reproduction can't practically use spot colors.",
