    shortDescription: "Accessible design means the work can be used and understood by people with a range of visual, motor, cognitive, or situational limitations - not an afterthought applied only...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Accessible design means the work can be used and understood by people with a range of visual, motor, cognitive, or situational limitations - not an afterthought applied only when explicitly requested. In practice, this means checking color contrast against WCAG guidelines (a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal body text, 3:1 for large text, against the AA standard most commonly targeted), never relying on color alone to convey information (adding icons, labels, or patterns alongside color-coded meaning for colorblind users), using a legible type size and clear hierarchy, and, for digital work, ensuring designs translate to a logical reading/tab order and meaningful alt text for images.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nAccessible design means the work can be used and understood by people with a range of visual, motor, cognitive, or situational limitations - not an afterthought applied only when explicitly requested. In practice, this means checking color contrast against WCAG guidelines (a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal body text, 3:1 for large text, against the AA standard most commonly targeted), never relying on color alone to convey information (adding icons, labels, or patterns alongside color-coded meaning for colorblind users), using a legible type size and clear hierarchy, and, for digital work, ensuring designs translate to a logical reading/tab order and meaningful alt text for images.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA dashboard using only red and green to indicate \"over budget\" versus \"on budget\" fails color-blind users who can't distinguish the two; adding a distinct icon (a warning triangle versus a checkmark) alongside the color fixes the underlying accessibility problem, not just the visual style.",
