    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Consistency starts with actually referencing the documented brand guidelines (colors, typography, logo usage, imagery style, tone) rather than working from memory or personal interpretation, and building or reusing shared templates/components for recurring deliverable types so each new piece doesn't require re-deriving the same decisions from scratch. When the guidelines are incomplete or ambiguous for a specific new situation, the right move is to make a reasoned extension consistent with the existing system and, where the client relationship allows it, confirm it with whoever owns the brand rather than guessing and potentially setting an inconsistent precedent that later work then copies.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nConsistency starts with actually referencing the documented brand guidelines (colors, typography, logo usage, imagery style, tone) rather than working from memory or personal interpretation, and building or reusing shared templates/components for recurring deliverable types so each new piece doesn't require re-deriving the same decisions from scratch. When the guidelines are incomplete or ambiguous for a specific new situation, the right move is to make a reasoned extension consistent with the existing system and, where the client relationship allows it, confirm it with whoever owns the brand rather than guessing and potentially setting an inconsistent precedent that later work then copies.\n\n**Example:**\n\nGiven a client's brand guideline documenting a primary color and two approved fonts but no explicit guidance for social media story templates, a designer would extend the existing type hierarchy and color usage logically to the new format, then flag the new usage to the brand owner for approval so it can be added to the guidelines going forward rather than becoming an undocumented one-off.",
