    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "A solid process generally moves through: clarifying the brief (audience, objective, constraints, what success looks like, and asking questions if anything's ambiguous rather than assuming); research and reference-gathering (competitors, existing brand material, relevant trends); rough concepting (several distinct directions, often as quick sketches or low-fidelity mockups, before committing to one); refining the strongest direction(s) with the actual content and details; gathering feedback at a meaningful checkpoint rather than only at the very end; and final production, including format-specific export and quality checks. The specific steps matter less than being able to explain why each step happened and how it fed into the next - an interviewer is checking for a deliberate, explainable process, not project-management jargon.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA solid process generally moves through: clarifying the brief (audience, objective, constraints, what success looks like, and asking questions if anything's ambiguous rather than assuming); research and reference-gathering (competitors, existing brand material, relevant trends); rough concepting (several distinct directions, often as quick sketches or low-fidelity mockups, before committing to one); refining the strongest direction(s) with the actual content and details; gathering feedback at a meaningful checkpoint rather than only at the very end; and final production, including format-specific export and quality checks. The specific steps matter less than being able to explain why each step happened and how it fed into the next - an interviewer is checking for a deliberate, explainable process, not project-management jargon.\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor a new event poster, the process might start with clarifying the target audience and key event details, then producing three distinct rough layout concepts, presenting those to the client for direction rather than a single finished piece, refining the chosen direction with final copy and imagery, and doing a final print-readiness check (bleed, color mode, resolution) before delivery.",
