    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Start by asking targeted clarifying questions rather than guessing or waiting passively - audience, objective, must-have constraints, examples of what they do and don't like, and what success looks like for this specific piece. Where some ambiguity remains even after that, translate it into a small number of genuinely different concept directions (not just variations of the same idea) so the client's reaction to seeing real options helps narrow the ambiguity faster than more discussion alone would. Treat a vague brief as a normal part of the job to actively manage, not a reason to wait for someone else to provide clarity before starting.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStart by asking targeted clarifying questions rather than guessing or waiting passively - audience, objective, must-have constraints, examples of what they do and don't like, and what success looks like for this specific piece. Where some ambiguity remains even after that, translate it into a small number of genuinely different concept directions (not just variations of the same idea) so the client's reaction to seeing real options helps narrow the ambiguity faster than more discussion alone would. Treat a vague brief as a normal part of the job to actively manage, not a reason to wait for someone else to provide clarity before starting.\n\n**Example:**\n\nGiven a brief that just says \"make it feel modern and trustworthy\" with no further detail, a designer might ask for two or three competitor examples the client admires or dislikes, then present two visually distinct directions - one cleaner and more minimal, one warmer and more editorial - using the client's reaction to each to converge on a clearer direction for the final piece.",
