    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Prioritize based on actual deadline proximity and dependency (is anyone else blocked waiting on this piece) rather than working on whichever task feels most interesting at the...",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Prioritize based on actual deadline proximity and dependency (is anyone else blocked waiting on this piece) rather than working on whichever task feels most interesting at the moment, and communicate proactively the moment it becomes clear something genuinely can't be done well within its deadline, rather than waiting until the deadline arrives to raise it. Time-blocking focused work on one project at a time, rather than constantly context-switching between several simultaneously, tends to produce better output than trying to make shallow progress on everything in parallel throughout the day.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPrioritize based on actual deadline proximity and dependency (is anyone else blocked waiting on this piece) rather than working on whichever task feels most interesting at the moment, and communicate proactively the moment it becomes clear something genuinely can't be done well within its deadline, rather than waiting until the deadline arrives to raise it. Time-blocking focused work on one project at a time, rather than constantly context-switching between several simultaneously, tends to produce better output than trying to make shallow progress on everything in parallel throughout the day.\n\n**Example:**\n\nWith three deliverables due the same week, a designer might identify that one is blocking a client's ad launch (highest priority), complete that first with focused time, flag early to the other two stakeholders that their pieces will follow by a specific revised time, and work through them in sequence rather than splitting attention across all three simultaneously and risking none being genuinely finished on time.",
