    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Use clear, consistent naming conventions for files and versions (avoiding ambiguous names like \"final_v2_ACTUALFINAL\"), organize layers and groups with descriptive names rather than default labels, keep a clean, logical layer/artboard structure rather than a flat pile of ungrouped elements, and separate working/source files from final exports. For handoff specifically, documenting key decisions (fonts used, color values, any non-obvious techniques) alongside the file, and using shared cloud storage or version control appropriate to the tool, avoids the common problem of a file that only makes sense to the person who originally built it.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nUse clear, consistent naming conventions for files and versions (avoiding ambiguous names like \"final_v2_ACTUALFINAL\"), organize layers and groups with descriptive names rather than default labels, keep a clean, logical layer/artboard structure rather than a flat pile of ungrouped elements, and separate working/source files from final exports. For handoff specifically, documenting key decisions (fonts used, color values, any non-obvious techniques) alongside the file, and using shared cloud storage or version control appropriate to the tool, avoids the common problem of a file that only makes sense to the person who originally built it.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA layered Photoshop file for a campaign is organized into clearly labeled groups (\"Background,\" \"Product Shot - Retouched,\" \"Headline Text,\" \"CTA Button\") with a version history and a brief notes layer listing the exact font names and hex color codes used, so a teammate covering for the original designer during vacation can make a requested text change without having to reverse-engineer the file structure first.",
