    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStructure the walkthrough around decisions, not just the finished visual: what was the actual brief and constraint, what alternative directions were considered and why they were set aside, what specific feedback shaped the final direction, and what the measurable or observed outcome was once it shipped, if known. Interviewers use this question specifically to separate genuine design reasoning from someone who can only describe what looks good - being ready to explain any single choice (a color, a layout decision, a typeface) in terms of the actual problem it was solving is what makes this answer land well.\n\n**Example:**\n\nRather than \"I designed this poster and I think it looks clean,\" a stronger walkthrough covers: the brief called for promoting a free community event to a broad, multigenerational audience; an initial illustration-heavy direction tested poorly with older attendees in an informal review, so the direction shifted to a simpler, high-contrast typographic layout with a single clear photo, which better served the actual audience and objective.",
