    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nLead with the project that best demonstrates the kind of work the role actually needs, not necessarily your personal favorite or most visually impressive piece - a role focused on brand systems benefits more from a system-building example than a one-off illustration, even if the illustration is the more striking image. Within each project, briefly frame the context (client, objective, constraints) before showing the work, then walk through key decisions and the outcome, rather than presenting a wall of final visuals with no narrative - a portfolio review is evaluating thinking and process at least as much as visual output.\n\n**Example:**\n\nInterviewing for an in-house brand-design role, a designer would lead with a project demonstrating a full identity system applied consistently across multiple deliverables, even if a separate, purely illustrative passion project is technically more visually striking, since it better demonstrates the actual skill the role needs.",
