    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "First assess concretely what's actually salvageable from the existing work versus what genuinely needs to restart, rather than assuming everything is wasted or nothing needs to change - a shift in copy direction might not require touching the underlying visual system, for instance. Communicate the realistic impact on timeline and scope clearly and promptly, since a brief change usually has cost implications the client needs to be aware of and agree to, rather than silently absorbing the extra work or unrealistic timeline pressure. Treat this as a normal, if frustrating, part of client work to manage professionally rather than a personal setback.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFirst assess concretely what's actually salvageable from the existing work versus what genuinely needs to restart, rather than assuming everything is wasted or nothing needs to change - a shift in copy direction might not require touching the underlying visual system, for instance. Communicate the realistic impact on timeline and scope clearly and promptly, since a brief change usually has cost implications the client needs to be aware of and agree to, rather than silently absorbing the extra work or unrealistic timeline pressure. Treat this as a normal, if frustrating, part of client work to manage professionally rather than a personal setback.\n\n**Example:**\n\nMidway through a rebrand project, a client decides they want a completely different visual direction. Rather than restarting from zero silently, a designer would assess that the underlying research and messaging strategy work is still valid, communicate that only the visual exploration phase needs to restart, and give a revised, realistic timeline reflecting that added work.",
