    sampleAnswer: "Rather than waiting for inspiration, use structured techniques to force forward movement: deliberately generating a larger quantity of rough, low-quality concepts quickly (which lowers the pressure on any single idea being good) often unblocks better ideas than trying to produce one polished concept directly; changing the input (new reference material, a different medium like sketching by hand instead of the computer, or briefly stepping away) can reset a stuck train of thought; and, under real time pressure, starting with whatever direction is safest and most defensible, and improving it iteratively, is often more productive than waiting for a stronger idea that may not arrive in time.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nRather than waiting for inspiration, use structured techniques to force forward movement: deliberately generating a larger quantity of rough, low-quality concepts quickly (which lowers the pressure on any single idea being good) often unblocks better ideas than trying to produce one polished concept directly; changing the input (new reference material, a different medium like sketching by hand instead of the computer, or briefly stepping away) can reset a stuck train of thought; and, under real time pressure, starting with whatever direction is safest and most defensible, and improving it iteratively, is often more productive than waiting for a stronger idea that may not arrive in time.\n\n**Example:**\n\nStuck on a packaging concept the night before a deadline, a designer might switch to quick hand-sketching instead of continuing to stare at the screen, produce ten rough, imperfect thumbnail ideas in twenty minutes rather than one polished concept, and often finds that the fifth or eighth rough idea, not the first, is the one worth developing further.",
