    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA strong answer shows a specific before-and-after with a clear reason for the change - what the original version got wrong or missed, what feedback or data revealed that, and what specifically was changed as a result - rather than a vague \"we iterated and it got better.\" This question is checking whether you treat your first draft as a hypothesis to test and refine rather than a finished answer to defend.\n\n**Example:**\n\n\"My first version of a signup form used a single long form on one page, and usability testing showed a high drop-off rate partway through. Breaking it into three shorter steps with a visible progress indicator, based directly on where users were dropping off, improved completion in a follow-up test - the change came directly from watching where real users struggled, not from a personal aesthetic preference.\"",
