    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThis is checking whether you connect design work to a measurable business outcome, not just visual quality. A strong answer names the actual business metric that was the real target (conversion rate, support ticket volume, time-on-task, brand recall) and traces a specific design change to a specific, ideally measured, improvement in that metric - showing that the design decision was made in service of that outcome, not the other way around.\n\n**Example:**\n\n\"A client's checkout page had a high abandonment rate. Rather than a visual refresh, I identified through the analytics that most drop-off happened right after shipping cost was revealed at the final step. Redesigning the flow to show shipping cost earlier, alongside simplifying the form itself, was the specific change tied to the actual business problem - and cart abandonment dropped measurably in the weeks after launch.\"",
